// A named binding inside a tuple case pattern, `(:ok, n: int64) -> ...` (S3.5 tuple_pattern,
// S4.1.7), inside an actor behaviour under a supervisor. Found 2026-09-18 while writing
// docs/learn-programming.md. Two halves: the type checker and SIR lowering used to reject `n` with
// E2002 "undefined identifier" (fixed 2026-09-18, each named tuple element is bound), and the emitter
// never matched a tuple pattern whose elements were not string literals or wildcards, so both
// messages fell through to the wildcard arm ("unmatched tuple pattern", then exit 70 on the second).
// The emitter half is fixed 2026-09-24 (term_case_patterns@emit_tuple_case_pattern_asm; the isolated
// form is case_addition/tuple_pattern_atom_element). The wildcard arm and the "done" marker keep the
// trial terminating under the supervisors harness. Expected: "cannot divide by zero", 20, done, exit 0.
========
// A tuple case pattern with a named binding inside a supervised behaviour: `(:ok, n: int64)` binds n
// across the arm's own calls (print_int64 is a call, and the binding lives in a frame slot for that
// reason). The front end has bound named tuple elements since 2026-09-18; the emitter matched such a
// pattern only from 2026-09-19 (see case_addition/tuple_pattern_atom_and_named_elements).
// Expected output: "cannot divide by zero", 20, done, exit 0.
